# Investigation and Response Flow

This diagram summarizes the analyst workflow from initial alert through containment and remediation.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["SIEM Alert<br/>Failed logons followed by success"]
    B["Validate Alert<br/>Confirm raw authentication events"]
    C["Validate Account and Asset<br/>sarnold / IT-LT-017"]
    D["Review Authentication History"]
    E["Pivot to Endpoint Processes"]
    F["Review PowerShell and<br/>administrative discovery"]
    G["Correlate Network Activity"]
    H["Check for Internal<br/>Follow-On Activity"]
    I["Review IT-WS-031"]
    J["Validate User and<br/>Device Context"]
    K{"Does activity remain<br/>reasonably benign?"}
    L["Document / Tune Detection"]
    M["Escalate Incident"]
    N["Isolate IT-LT-017"]
    O["Secure sarnold Account"]
    P["Validate Secondary Scope"]
    Q["Preserve Evidence"]
    R["Remediate and Recover"]
    S["Post-Incident Review"]

    A --> B
    B --> C
    C --> D
    D --> E
    E --> F
    F --> G
    G --> H
    H --> I
    I --> J
    J --> K

    K -->|Yes| L
    K -->|No| M

    M --> N
    M --> O
    N --> P
    O --> P
    P --> Q
    Q --> R
    R --> S
```

## Workflow Principle

The investigation moves outward from the original alert rather than assuming the initial event already proves compromise.

Containment begins only after authentication, process, network, and user context are sufficiently correlated to justify disruptive action.

The same approach limits unnecessary business impact while still allowing the SOC to move quickly when the evidence becomes strong.
